           <h2 class="post-title">Visual Studio 2012 Quick Launch is Awesome!</h2>
              <div class="post-content clearfix">
    	       <p>Those who know have coded with me have felt the soul crushing comments whenever they touch a mouse.&#160; It. Must. Die.&#160; For coders at least.&#160; Visual Studio hasn’t always made that easy.&#160; Visual Studio 2012 is here to help with the new Quick Launch feature. (<a href="https://login.live.com/login.srf?wa=wsignin1.0&amp;wtrealm=visualstudiogallery.msdn.microsoft.com&amp;wreply=https%3a%2f%2fvisualstudiogallery.msdn.microsoft.com%2f59ca71b3-a4a3-46ca-8fe1-0e90e3f79329%3fstoAI%3d10&amp;wp=MBI_FED_SSL&amp;wlcxt=microsoft%24microsoft%24microsoft" target="_blank">VsVim</a> will help too, but in due time).</p>
<p>Quick Launch is found in what used to be the window chrome.</p>
<p><a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image.png"><img style="background-image: none; border-right-width: 0px; margin: 4px 10px; padding-left: 0px; padding-right: 0px; display: inline; border-top-width: 0px; border-bottom-width: 0px; border-left-width: 0px; padding-top: 0px" title="image" border="0" alt="image" src="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image_thumb.png" width="644" height="413" /></a></p>
<p>Stop that!&#160; Don’t go mousing up there just yet.&#160; It quite blaringly asks you to hit CTRL+Q.&#160; Just do it.&#160; Now type ‘Keyboard’</p>
<p>Holy frick!&#160; It took us into a section of the Tools –&gt; Options dialog.&#160; That’s a deep link if ever one existed.<a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image1.png"><img style="background-image: none; border-right-width: 0px; margin: 4px 10px; padding-left: 0px; padding-right: 0px; display: inline; border-top-width: 0px; border-bottom-width: 0px; border-left-width: 0px; padding-top: 0px" title="image" border="0" alt="image" src="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image_thumb1.png" width="644" height="262" /></a></p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p><strong>Open to all comers</strong></p>
<p>What if we search for ‘gist’?</p>
<p><a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image2.png"><img style="background-image: none; border-right-width: 0px; margin: 4px 10px; padding-left: 0px; padding-right: 0px; display: inline; border-top-width: 0px; border-bottom-width: 0px; border-left-width: 0px; padding-top: 0px" title="image" border="0" alt="image" src="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image_thumb2.png" width="644" height="165" /></a></p>
<p>Yeah, that’s my <a href="visualstudiogallery.msdn.microsoft.com/b31916b0-c026-4c27-9d6b-ba831093f6b2" target="_blank">Gister</a> VS Extension.&#160; I didn’t have to do anything to play in the sandbox.&#160; Resharper refactorings, TestDriven.Net, and Telerik’s tools all show up side-by-side commands like the new backed in Continuous Test Runner.</p>
<p><a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image3.png"><img style="background-image: none; border-right-width: 0px; padding-left: 0px; padding-right: 0px; display: inline; border-top-width: 0px; border-bottom-width: 0px; border-left-width: 0px; padding-top: 0px" title="image" border="0" alt="image" src="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image_thumb3.png" width="244" height="217" /></a></p>
<p><strong>Make your own keyboard combos</strong></p>
<p>Also notice that the respective keyboard combos are called out clearly.&#160; If you find yourself using Quick Launch often for the same command, setup a specific shortcut. </p>
<p><a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image4.png"><img style="background-image: none; border-right-width: 0px; padding-left: 0px; padding-right: 0px; display: inline; border-top-width: 0px; border-bottom-width: 0px; border-left-width: 0px; padding-top: 0px" title="image" border="0" alt="image" src="http://cromwellhaus.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/image_thumb4.png" width="644" height="168" /></a></p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p>Note that Quick Launch is context sensitive.&#160; Commands that are not available (i.e. Resharper Rename refactoring in a non-code file) will not appear in the search. </p>
